<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE topic
        SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/xhtml-entities.dtd">
<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       title="Quick jump alias" id="Quick-jump-alias">

    <p>
        <code>%tool_call% search</code> allow you to jump to repositories quickly.
    </p>

    <chapter title="Installation process" id="completion_chapter-search-alias">

        <p>
            <code>%tool_call% search</code> has its own <code>--completion</code> flag. It prints a small function,
            named <code>gcd</code> by default.
        </p>

        <procedure title="Install gcd">
            <step>
                Add the function to your shell startup file.
                <tabs>
                    <tab title="Bash">
                        <code-block lang="bash"><![CDATA[
gogws search --completion bash >> ~/.bashrc
]]></code-block>
                    </tab>
                    <tab title="Zsh">
                        <code-block lang="bash"><![CDATA[
gogws search --completion zsh >> ~/.zshrc
]]></code-block>
                    </tab>
                    <tab title="Fish">
                        <code-block lang="shell"><![CDATA[
gogws search --completion fish >> ~/.config/fish/config.fish
]]></code-block>
                    </tab>
                    <tab title="PowerShell">
                        <code-block lang="powershell"><![CDATA[
gogws search --completion powershell >> $PROFILE
]]></code-block>
                    </tab>
                </tabs>
            </step>
            <step>Open a new terminal.</step>
        </procedure>

        <p>
            Use <code>--alias</code> to choose other names, for example
            <code>%tool_call% search --completion zsh --alias gcd,smartcd,goto,...</code>.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Jump with gcd" id="scope_chapter-gcd">
        <p>
            <code>gcd</code> is a shell function: it runs <code>%tool_call% search --cd</code> and changes to the
            result.
        </p>
        <code-block lang="shell"><![CDATA[
$ gcd infra-tools
$ pwd
/work/top/mid/deep/infra-tools
]]></code-block>

        <tip>
            You can use <code>--scope</code> with the <code>gcd</code> function to specify the scope of the search.
        </tip>

        <p><code>--cd</code> prints the path instead of the table:</p>
        <list>
            <li>One match: the path is printed and <code>gcd</code> changes to it.</li>
            <li>Several matches: you pick one in a list. This needs an interactive terminal.</li>
            <li>No match: an error, and you stay where you are.</li>
        </list>
    </chapter>
</topic>
